---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro'
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro'
import Steps from '../../components/Steps.astro'
import { EnvReplica } from '../../components/app/Replicas'
---

<Guide
  slug="env-files"
  title="Storing a .env"
  description="Import a .env file into the vault, keep it byte for byte, and copy one key or the whole file."
  lede="A .env is usually the most dangerous file in a project and the one treated most carelessly. This is the module Sanctum was really built for."
>
  <h2>The problem it solves</h2>
  <p>
    Your <code>.env</code> holds the production database URI, the Stripe secret key, the AWS
    credentials. It sits in plaintext in a project folder, gets pasted into chat when a teammate
    needs it, and gets copied to a second machine by whatever means is nearest. Nobody decided
    that; it just accumulated.
  </p>
  <p>
    A password manager does not help, because a <code>.env</code> is not a login. It is a file, and
    it needs to come back out as the same file.
  </p>

  <h2>Adding one</h2>
  <Steps steps={[
    'Open <strong>Env Files</strong> and click <strong>+</strong>.',
    'Name it after the project &mdash; the client or repository name, not the filename. All three environments for a project share that name.',
    'Choose the environment: production, staging or local.',
    'Click <strong>Import .env</strong> and pick the file, or paste the contents into the box directly.',
    'Save. What you saved is stored exactly as written.',
  ]} />

  <EnvReplica />

  <Callout kind="note" title="Import reads the file in Rust, not in the page">
    <p>
      Picking a file opens the ordinary Windows dialog, and the path goes to the Rust side, which
      reads it. The interface layer never gains filesystem access of its own &mdash; it cannot name
      a path you did not choose. Files over 1&nbsp;MB and files that are not valid UTF-8 text are
      refused rather than stored as mangled text.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Why byte for byte matters</h2>
  <p>
    Sanctum stores the raw file text and parses it only to decide what to show you. The parser has
    no authority over what is saved.
  </p>
  <p>
    That distinction is the whole design. A store that kept parsed key-value pairs would drop your
    <code># rotated 2026-03</code> comments, lose blank-line grouping, and reorder the keys. Pasting
    it back into a fresh checkout would give you a file that <em>works</em> &mdash; which is what
    makes that failure mode nasty, because you would not notice until you needed the comment.
  </p>
  <p>
    Comments, blank lines, quoting style, key order and even CRLF line endings all survive
    unchanged.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading and copying</h2>
  <ul class="plainList">
    <li><strong>Values are masked</strong> when you open a record. The key names are visible; the values are dots until you reveal them individually.</li>
    <li><strong>Copy one value</strong> when a client rotates a single key &mdash; the common case, and the one that usually means selecting text by hand elsewhere.</li>
    <li><strong>Copy the whole file</strong> when setting up a machine. This always sends the stored text, never a reconstruction from the parsed rows.</li>
    <li>Both go through the same guarded clipboard the Vault uses: excluded from Windows history and cloud sync, cleared after thirty seconds.</li>
  </ul>

  <Callout kind="note" title="When the parser cannot read your file">
    <p>
      <code>.env</code> has no specification and every tool disagrees at the edges. If Sanctum
      cannot find any <code>KEY=value</code> lines, it shows the raw text with a short note instead
      of an error, and the whole-file copy still returns it exactly.
    </p>
    <p>
      Multi-line values &mdash; a PEM private key, most often &mdash; are handled: a quoted value
      spanning several lines is read as one entry. If something still confuses the parser, only the
      display is affected. What you saved is what you get back.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Organising by project</h2>
  <p>
    The environment is a field on the record, not a folder, so one project's production, staging
    and local files sit together in the list rather than scattered across three folders. Filter the
    list by environment when you want only one.
  </p>
  <p>
    For grouping by client, create a folder under <strong>Folders &rarr; Env Files</strong> and
    file records into it. As with credentials, those folders are separate from the Vault's.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="warn" title="What this does not do">
    <p>
      Sanctum stores <code>.env</code> files; it does not inject them. There is no command that
      feeds secrets into a running process, so the file still lands on disk in the project when you
      paste it back.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is a real limitation, and a deliberate scope line rather than an oversight.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Guide>
